  "announcements": [
    {
      "title": "Class 23: Approve or decline?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-11-17T21:25:42Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Nov 17, 2026, 4:25 PM EST",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1428357",
      "body": "Class 23: Approve or decline?\nThe forest gives each application a probability of default â€” the question is where to draw the line. The cutoff is the officer's policy, priced from two mistakes: a missed default vs. a rejected good loan. At a cutoff of 0.167, the mistakes cost $35.1M â€” against $64.9M for \"approve all\" and $55.3M for a one-line rule. The model is good enough when it beats what needs no model.\nNext class (Thu 11/19): verification lab.\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\ns23 Â· Predict a number: the Eastville seller (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/s23-star-predict-a-number-the-eastville-seller) â€” the same cutoff logic when the outcome is a number, not a 1/0.\ns23 Â· A different decider: the loan auditor (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/s23-star-a-different-decider-the-loan-auditor) â€” the same model, a different person acting on it.\nYour memo, notebook, and slides are due Wed 12/02, 11:59 PM."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 22: What does the model say?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-11-13T01:42:00Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Nov 12, 2026, 8:42 PM EST",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1426202",
      "body": "Class 22: What does the model say?\nThe head of the desk asked: which columns does the model use, and what does it say? A tree and a forest answer with feature importance â€” which columns matter, with no direction. Logistic has no such list; its coefficients compare units, not importance, so you read the odds ratio and the range ratio instead. A partial-dependence plot shows the full shape. Two models on the same loans can disagree on a column â€” that's a finding, not a bug.\nNext class (Tue 11/17): the Answer session.\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nS22 homework â€” What does the model say? (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/quizzes/299483) â€” due Tue 11/17, 10:00 AM. Read what two models say about the loan columns; decide which claims the desk can sign."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 21: Two ways a perfect score lies",
      "posted_utc": "2026-11-10T23:41:41Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Nov 10, 2026, 6:41 PM EST",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1425039",
      "body": "Class 21: Two ways a perfect score lies\nThe agent's decision tree got every loan right â€” 16,208 of 16,208. It was scored on the same loans it learned from. Today we cut the file in two: train on 70%, score on the 30% the tree never saw. The gap between those two scores is overfitting â€” a model that memorized instead of learned â€” and max_depth is the limit that closes it. A perfect score is not a sign to deploy; sometimes it's the warning.\nNext class (Thu 11/12): interpreting the model â€” feature importance and odds ratios.\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nS21 homework â€” A tree and a forest on new loans (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/quizzes/299331) â€” due Thu 11/12, 10:00 AM. Fit a tree and a forest on the decision-day columns; find the overfit and the leak."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 20: Move the line and every number moves",
      "posted_utc": "2026-11-06T02:23:52Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Nov 5, 2026, 9:23 PM EST",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1421785",
      "body": "Class 20: Move the line and every number moves\n\nTuesday's model gave each loan a probability; a line at 0.5 turned it into a call. Today we moved that line and put a price on each mistake. The 0.5 line is a default nobody chose: the most accurate line (83%), and it costs $208.5M. Move it to 0.16 and the model catches 1,797 of 2,650 defaulters for $146.8M. A measure describes a line; it can't choose one. The cost does.\n\nNext class (Tue 11/10): train/test split â€” and you'll learn your go or no-go on your scope.\n\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings). Before Sunday, walk through the airline (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/scoping-the-airline-project-one-file-two-questions) and hotel (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/scoping-the-hotel-project-one-file-two-questions) scoping examples â€” one file, two questions, the same five checks you'll run.\n\nAfter class assignments\n\nCycle 2 Â· Team scope â€” your question, your file (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1318262) â€” due Sun 11/08, 11:59 PM."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 19: How many defaulters would the desk have caught?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-11-03T23:22:40Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Nov 3, 2026, 6:22 PM EST",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1418612",
      "body": "Class 19: How many defaulters would the desk have caught?\nThe agent built the desk's first default model and called it 83% accurate â€” but \"approve everyone\" scores 83.7%, because only 16% of loans default. The model caught 112 defaulters; the desk's one-line DTI rule caught 477. A probability is not a decision.\nNext class (Thu 11/05): we scope two projects, live.\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings). The final project brief (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/final-project-what-you-deliver-and-how-it-is-graded) is on Canvas â€” what you deliver and how it's graded.\nAfter class assignments\n\nS19 homework â€” Can we trust the model's probabilities? (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1319637) â€” due Thu 11/05, 10:00 AM. Where you can trust the model's probability, and where not.\nCycle 2 Â· Team scope â€” your question, your file (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1318262) â€” due Sun 11/08, 11:59 PM. One per team; no scope by the deadline â†’ an assigned project."
    },
    {
      "title": "Personal Identity & Networking in the Digital Age â€” Sunday, Nov 8, 1:00â€“4:30 PM",
      "posted_utc": "2026-11-02T03:45:45Z",
      "posted_local": "Sun Nov 1, 2026, 10:45 PM EST",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1417583",
      "body": "This Sunday, November 8 (1:00â€“4:30 PM), the Futures Forum with ISOM is hosting Personal Identity & Networking in the Digital Age in the Boynton Auditorium.\n\nWith internships, first jobs, and career transitions ahead, this is about more than a rÃ©sumÃ© â€” it's about building a credible personal identity online and networking deliberately, rather than settling for a shallow social-media presence or a thin vita. Three speakers on content creation and networking will share their perspectives, joined by several alumni discussing their recent journeys.\n\nEvent handout (PDF) (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/files/17283843)\n\nOpen to students regardless of program. We hope to see you there."
    },
    {
      "title": "Appointment sign-ups â€” next week is invite-only",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-29T22:36:21Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Oct 29, 2026, 6:36 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1416944",
      "body": "Hi everyone â€” a quick heads-up about the appointment slots.\nWeek of Nov 2 â€” \"352 Midterm Review\": these slots are reserved for a specific group of students I'll be reaching out to directly. If you've signed up for this week and haven't heard from me, please release your slot so those students can book it.\nWeek of Nov 9 â€” \"352 Midterm Open\": this week is open to everyone â€” please sign up there instead.\nThanks for helping keep this fair!"
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 18: Where did the 2019 book lose money?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-29T19:30:42Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Oct 29, 2026, 3:30 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1416854",
      "body": "Class 18: Where did the 2019 book lose money?\nThe 2019 book has 20,000 loans and $6.64B, and 24.6% of them defaulted. One query claimed EQUI-scored loans default 99.95% of the time. The numbers were right â€” but a JOIN row is a match, not a loan: 25,547 rows for 20,000 loans, and a column that predicts the outcome almost perfectly is usually a leaked label. A join's proof line is COUNT(DISTINCT id).\nNext class (Tue 11/03): the flat file, and the first model.\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nS18 homework â€” Peachtree orders, counted once (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1318203) â€” due Tue 11/03, 10:00 AM. Run SQL in the sandbox and check the join counts before you sign them.\nCycle 2 Â· Team scope â€” your question, your file (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1318262) â€” due Wed 11/04, 11:59 PM. One per team â€” your question, your file, and the Ask Card sentence."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 17: The row count was right. The table wasn't.",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-27T20:11:35Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Oct 27, 2026, 4:11 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1415966",
      "body": "Class 17: The row count was right. The table wasn't.\nThe API was down, so the agent read all 108 listing pages and had an LLM turn each into a row. The proof line passed â€” 108 = 108. But a row count can't see inside a cell: one misread price moved the gap $125, and one unwritten definition of \"basement\" moved it $22,000. Rules you can read; a model you can't.\nNext class (Thu 10/29): the loan file â€” who defaults?\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nCycle 2 group formation (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1316287) â€” due Tue 10/27, 11:59 PM. Five per team; no preference â†’ submit \"no preference\"."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 16: The number was right. The table was too small.",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-22T20:56:10Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Oct 22, 2026, 4:56 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1414163",
      "body": "Class 16: The number was right. The table was too small.\nThe agent pulled Eastville from the broker's API and signed a $6,220 basement discount. The code ran clean every time â€” but it had pulled only page 1: 16 of 108 homes. Pulling every page moved the gap to $65,160. A correct number from a file that isn't the world isn't a claim about the world.\nNext class (Tue 10/27): SQL â€” the same questions, a different language.\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nCycle 2: Ask â€” Your Individual Question (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1413012) â€” due Sun 10/25. Add to your post: where your data comes from (a link) and one audit question (check 1, 2, or 3).\nS16 homework â€” One year of Chicago food inspections (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1316754) â€” due Tue 10/27, 10:00 AM. Pull a full year of Chicago food inspections and sign the fail rate.\nCycle 2 group formation (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1316287) â€” due Tue 10/27, 11:59 PM. Five per team; no preference â†’ submit \"no preference\"."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 15: Why do most projects die before they start?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-20T18:13:08Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Oct 20, 2026, 2:13 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1413017",
      "body": "Class 15: Why do most projects die before they start?\nThree teams wrote a project brief last year. Two of the three died. Today you read those briefs and found the exact check each one failed â€” the Ask Card's five checks, plus the gate. From now on that card is how you audit your own question before it becomes a project.\nNext class (Thu 10/22): team meeting. Bring your question's sentence.\nSlides and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings). The Ask Card field guide (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/the-ask-card-field-guide-page-1) and the loan file (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/s15-star-the-loan-file) are on Canvas.\nAfter class assignments\n\nCycle 2: Ask â€” Your Individual Question (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1413012) â€” post your question as a comment. Due Sun 10/25, sections 1â€“4, no file yet.\nCycle 2 group formation (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1316287) â€” due Tue 10/27, 11:59 PM. Five per team; no preference â†’ submit \"no preference\"."
    },
    {
      "title": "ğŸ“ Exam Today (Thu 10/15) â€” Canvas Quiz + LockDown Browser",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-15T04:54:22Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Oct 15, 2026, 12:54 AM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1411186",
      "body": "A reminder that today's exam (Thursday, October 15) will be taken on Canvas as a quiz, using Respondus LockDown Browser. ğŸ”’\n\nPlease make sure to bring your electronic device (laptop) â€” you'll need it to access the quiz. ğŸ’»\n\nInstall LockDown Browser before you arrive, and update it if prompted.\n\nClose other applications before starting.\n\nArrive a few minutes early so we can start on time.\n\nGood luck â€” you've got this! ğŸ’ª"
    },
    {
      "title": "ğŸ“… Midterm Office Hours â€” Wed 10/14 Â· 10:00 AMâ€“1:30 PM Â· GBS 415",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-14T00:42:02Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Oct 13, 2026, 8:42 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1410650",
      "body": "I'll be holding office hours this Wednesday, October 14, from 10:00 AM to 1:30 PM in GBS 415, mainly for midterm questions. ğŸ—“ï¸\n\nStop by with anything you want to review before the midterm â€” no appointment needed, just drop in. ğŸ‘‹"
    },
    {
      "title": "Practice for Exam 1: one more file to audit",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-09T04:32:32Z",
      "posted_local": "Fri Oct 9, 2026, 12:32 AM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1409628",
      "body": "So far you have worked through the same process on two different files:\n\nReal estate (Eastville). The example we used in class to learn the process. The question: what is a basement worth?\n\nRetail banking (Lab 1). The example you practised on. The question: are online customers more profitable? Suggested answers: Stages A and B (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/lab-1-stages-a-and-b-with-suggested-answers) and Stage C (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/lab-1-stage-c-with-suggested-answers).\n\nHere is a third, for practice before the exam:\n\nStore staffing. A convenience-store chain asks: do stores whose managers and crew have more experience earn more profit?\n\nThe file and the business are new. The work is the same: an AI agent has done the analysis, and you decide which of its claims you would sign.\n\nPractice quiz: Practice for Exam 1 Â· Audit the agent on the store file (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/quizzes/297317). Nine claims. Give yourself 30 minutes, work alone, and keep AI off for your first attempt. It is not graded and you can take it more than once.\n\nSuggested answers: The store file, with suggested answers (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/practice-for-exam-1-the-store-file-with-suggested-answers). Each answer is hidden until you open it. Take the quiz first, then compare your reasons with ours.\n\nExam 1 is Thursday, October 15, in class: this same task, alone, with no AI.\n\nWenjun Gu, PhD"
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 13: Which claim would your team sign?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-08T21:21:32Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Oct 8, 2026, 5:21 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1409569",
      "body": "Class 13: Which claim would your team sign?\n\nThe agent made three claims about the model; today your team decided which to sign â€” then saw the answers and spent twenty minutes on the memo. The sharpest find: \"district has no effect\" wasn't a finding, it was a coding choice. Fed in as one number, district washed out; as dummies, district 1200 is worth about $19. Two in three of you could read the line; one in four used it. Reading the code isn't checking it.\n\nNext class (Thu 10/15): Exam 1.\n\nNotes, scripts, and the recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\n\nAfter class assignments\n\nPeer evaluation â€” cycle 1 (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1312559) â€” due Fri 10/9, 12:45 PM.\n\nTeam memo â€” what we tell our decider (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1312557) â€” due Fri 10/9, 11:59 PM. One PDF per team.\n\nCycle 1 Ledger â€” the closer (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1303909) â€” due Sun 10/11, 11:59 PM."
    },
    {
      "title": "Lab 1 practice page: Stages A and B, with suggested answers",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-08T00:34:39Z",
      "posted_local": "Wed Oct 7, 2026, 8:34 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1409125",
      "body": "Several of you asked for the answers to Tuesday's lab. Stages A and B are now on a practice page: Lab 1: Stages A and B, with suggested answers (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/lab-1-stages-a-and-b-with-suggested-answers).\n\nThe page has the agent's code, its output, its five claims, and all 14 questions. Each suggested answer is hidden until you open it.\n\nIf you missed Tuesday's class, do this before Thursday. Set aside about 30 minutes, keep AI off, and answer each question before you open its answer. That is the closest you can get to the lab itself. The page is for practice only. The in-class quizzes are closed and cannot be made up.\n\nIf you were in class, use the page to check your reasoning, not only your verdicts. A right verdict for the wrong reason will not hold up on the exam.\n\nStage C is not on this page. Your team works on it together on Thursday, and its answers will be posted after that.\n\nExam 1 is Thursday 10/15. It is the same task: an agent's notebook, and you decide what you would sign, alone and without AI."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 12: Are online customers more profitable?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-06T18:43:55Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Oct 6, 2026, 2:43 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1408540",
      "body": "Class 12: Are online customers more profitable?\n\nToday you audited an AI agent's work on a bank's customer file, alone and with no AI: eight claims across three stages, sign or don't sign. The first two stages came down to asking who was removed before trusting what is left, and to a $5 gap that hid a real difference inside each age group. Some claims were fine, and signing those counted too. Stage C stays sealed.\n\nNext class (Thu 10/8): your team settles Stage C before anyone sees the answers. Then the memo, finished at your table. Exam 1 is Thu 10/15.\n\nSlides are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\n\nAfter class assignments\n\nLab 1 Â· After class â€” audit the agent (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/quizzes/297125) â€” due Thu 10/8, 10:00 AM. AI on: send Stage B to an AI and grade its answer.\n\nTeam memo â€” what we tell our decider (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1312557) â€” due Fri 10/9, 11:59 PM. One per team; bring a draft Thursday."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 11: One number, three readers",
      "posted_utc": "2026-10-02T03:36:21Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Oct 1, 2026, 11:36 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1406931",
      "body": "Class 11: One number, three readers\nToday you stopped computing and started telling. The same basement number â€” $18K, give or take âˆ’$8K to $44K â€” means something different to the buyer, the seller, and the listing agent. It's a comparison between the 91 homes with a basement and the 17 without â€” not what adding one returns. A number that moves goes to your decider as a range, with which homes and which columns.\nNext class (Tue 10/6): a stranger's notebook on a file you've never seen.\nSlides, notebook, and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nThe team memo â€” due Fri 10/9, 11:59 PM. One page to your decider: Â§2 from s10, Â§4 from today's STOP 2."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 10: Which model do you sign?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-29T18:24:36Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Sep 29, 2026, 2:24 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1405705",
      "body": "Class 10: Which model do you sign?\n\nToday you stopped letting the agent pick. Backward elimination dropped the basement (p = .142) and called it done â€” but removed is not zero. You built your own from four models: 108 or 97 rows, garage in or out. The basement came back $18K to $30K â€” three of four can't tell it from zero â€” while a square foot held at $110 to $145 in every one.\n\nNext class (Thu 10/1): an agent's notebook on a file you've never seen â€” and you, alone.\n\nSlides, notebook, and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\n\nAfter class assignments\n\nS10 homework â€” The school zone, four ways (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/quizzes/296869) â€” due Thu 10/1, 10:00 AM. Read Apple Valley off each of the four models and write the memo sentence."
    },
    {
      "title": "Paid fellowship â€” help redesign ISOM 352 for the AI era",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-28T05:44:12Z",
      "posted_local": "Mon Sep 28, 2026, 1:44 AM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1404750",
      "body": "Paid fellowship â€” help redesign ISOM 352 for the AI era\n\nISOM 352 is being rebuilt around one question: when an AI agent can run the Python â†’ pandas â†’ regression stack for you, what's left for a human to be good at? My answer is specification and audit literacy â€” framing a problem precisely, and knowing when the agent's answer is wrong.\n\nI'm looking for one BBA student to co-design and pilot the new materials as a Robson Fellow â€” a paid role (stipend funded by the Robson Innovation in Research and Teaching Grant). You'd help build agent-based exercises where the AI deliberately produces plausible-but-wrong output and students learn to catch it.\n\nYou're a fit if you're comfortable in Python, use AI tools heavily (especially if you've hit that \"the agent gave me an answer and I'm not sure it's right\" feeling), and want to reshape how business education works in an AI world. Any background is welcome â€” you just need to be a BBA student.\n\nTo apply (short is fine), email wenjun.gu@emory.edu (mailto:wenjun.gu@emory.edu) with the subject line \"Robson Fellow application\":\n\nName, program, year\n\n2â€“3 sentences on why this interests you\n\nA line on your Python comfort and how you actually use AI in your work\n\nResume/CV (required)\n\nDeadline: end of day Tuesday, September 29. I have to name the Fellow in a grant packet this week, so I can't consider late entries."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 9: Which premium survives?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-24T18:39:09Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Sep 24, 2026, 2:39 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1403901",
      "body": "Class 9: Which premium survives?\nToday you held the house fixed and watched the premiums collapse. The fireplace's $52K became $8K and vanished. The basement's $65K shrank to $18K â€” but its interval still touched $0, so you couldn't sign it. Only Apple Valley survived, at $27K. A number has a give-or-take, and the give-or-take is what you sign.\nNext class (Tue 9/29): five models, one basement â€” does its fate depend on the model?\nSlides, notebook, and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nTeam ledger, part 2 â€” the basement, held fixed (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1310653) â€” due Tue 9/29, 10:00 AM. Five models, one basement; one team submission."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 8: What is a square foot worth?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-22T18:40:08Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Sep 22, 2026, 2:40 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1402812",
      "body": "Class 8: What is a square foot worth?\nToday you drew a line through Eastville's prices and read off $143 â€” what one more square foot is worth, not the $171 a whole home averages per square foot. Then you refused to let that line speak for a 4,000-square-foot home it had never seen. A number is only right for the question it was asked.\nNext class (Thu 9/24): add the controls â€” the baths, fireplaces, and bigger Apple Valley homes that come bundled with a square foot.\nSlides, notebook, and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nS8 homework â€” Whose homes is the line standing on? (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/quizzes/296644) â€” due Thu 9/24, 10:00 AM. Refit the line without House 108, then check the eleven homes missing an age."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 7: Which picture is telling the truth?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-17T21:09:22Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Sep 17, 2026, 5:09 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1401068",
      "body": "Class 7: Which picture is telling the truth?\nToday the agent drew the buyer's â€œwhich zoneâ€ answer, then â€œtidiedâ€ one chart by starting its axis at $280,000 instead of $0 â€” small gaps looked enormous. You refused the captions that didn't survive the check, fixed the chart in as few lines as you could, and chose bar or box for the memo. Keep the two words that start every honest answer: it depends â€” mean or median? On the skewness. Mild or heavy? Each â€œdependsâ€ is a step, not a stall.\nNext class (Tue 9/22): 500 more square feet is worth what?\nSlides, notebook, and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\nAfter class assignments\n\nS7 homework â€” The fireplace, three ways (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/quizzes/296453) â€” due Tue 9/22, 10:00 AM. The gap, the reasons, and the chart for the fireplace; AI on, with disclosure."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 6: Is it the basement, or is it the house?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-15T22:11:20Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Sep 15, 2026, 6:11 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1400119",
      "body": "Today you ran the basement's number the way the agent ran the fireplace's â€” same groupby, and it came back $65,160 more for basement homes on the mean. Then the one-line check that would have caught day one: run the groupby on everything else. The homes aren't otherwise-similar â€” 3.7 vs 3.3 bedrooms, 2.7 vs 2.4 baths, twice as likely in Apple Valley. You sign \"sell for more,\" not \"adds\" â€” the $65K is the basement and everything that comes with it.\n\nNext class (Thu 9/17): the picture version â€” what does a chart catch that the table hides?\n\nSlides, notebook, and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\n\nAfter class assignments\n\nS6 homework â€” Hold one thing equal (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/quizzes/296380) â€” due Thu 9/17, 10:00 AM. Run the slices yourself and decide how small is too small."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 5: What is a typical Eastville home worth?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-10T21:14:29Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Sep 10, 2026, 5:14 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1398176",
      "body": "Today you wrote the first sentence of a memo and found the hard part is the word, not the number. Price's mean ($341,395) sits $9,680 above the median â€” four homes over $500K drag it up â€” while square footage has no tail, so its mean and median agree. Same statistic, honest once and misleading once. For categories, \"most common\" names the biggest group â€” \"most\" claims more than half, so check the share first. Look before you choose.\n\nNext class (Tue 9/15): two variables â€” what moves with what?\n\nSlides, notebook, and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\n\nAfter class assignments\n\nTeam startup â€” one call, one ledger (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1306701) â€” due Tue 9/15, 10:00 AM. One submission per team, all five names.\n\nGroup members: check Cycle 1 Groups (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1305422) to find your team."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 4: Three rows were lying",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-08T20:31:44Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Sep 8, 2026, 4:31 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1397008",
      "body": "Class 4: Three rows were lying\n\nThursday the vendor said 108 homes; the file said 111. Three houses were listed twice â€” drop the copies, you're at 108. Then the harder call: 11 homes are missing age and run about $39K cheaper. You made the drop-or-keep call in class. We didn't reach STOP 3, so run the rest of the notebook â€” including saving eastville_clean.csv â€” before the homework.\n\nNext class (Thu 9/10): how do you describe a whole neighborhood with one number â€” and know when that number lies?\n\nSlides, notebook, and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\n\nAfter class assignments\n\nS4 homework â€” The missing age (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1306247) â€” due Thu 9/10, 10:00 AM. Test today's drop-or-keep call against more evidence."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 3: Does the file clear the gate?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-03T18:13:24Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Sep 3, 2026, 2:13 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1395033",
      "body": "Class 3: Does the file clear the gate?\n\nThe Eastville file arrived and immediately refused: price came in as text, so a one-line average crashed. You fixed it and proved the fix with dtype â€” the typical home is worth about $333K (median; the mean is ~$342K, pulled up by a few expensive houses). Then you checked the other columns: quantity or label?\n\nNext class (Tue 9/8): the three extra rows have names. Which ones?\n\nNotes and recording are on the notes and recordings page (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings).\n\nAfter class assignments\n\nRhythm Portfolio Â· Week 02 (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1292608) â€” due Sun 9/6, 11:59 PM. Three short entries, AI off.\n\nComplete and implement all codes in the s3 notebook."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 2: What is a basement worth?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-09-01T19:41:38Z",
      "posted_local": "Tue Sep 1, 2026, 3:41 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1393647",
      "body": "Today was the Ask stage â€” before any data, and where most questions die. What you ask is the whole game: question â†’ decision â†’ data, in that order â€” a decider, an outcome column, and an \"otherwise-similar\" list. You went screens-down on the basement, wrote your own spec, and audited a neighbor's. From now on, every No names the check that would change it.\n\nNext class (Thu 9/3): the file â€” 108 homes.\n\nSlides and recording are on the Notes, scripts, and recordings (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings) page.\n\nAfter class assignments:\n\nS2 after class (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1303925) â€” due Thu 9/3, 8:00 AM. An AI attacks your spec; send back the best attack and your fix."
    },
    {
      "title": "Class 1: What is a fireplace worth in Eastville?",
      "posted_utc": "2026-08-27T20:46:29Z",
      "posted_local": "Thu Aug 27, 2026, 4:46 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1391312",
      "body": "Today you ran an AI agent's analysis, signed off on its $52,776 fireplace premium, and then couldn't find what was wrong with it. Homes with a fireplace are simply bigger â€” the number answers a question nobody asked. That's the course: the agent writes the code; you're accountable for the number.\n\nNext class (Tue 9/1): What is a basement worth? Bring a pen.\n\nHere is the after class Notes, scripts, and recordings (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/pages/notes-scripts-and-recordings) \n\nAfter class assignments:\n\nRhythm Portfolio Â· Week 01 (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1292604) â€” Entry 0 â€” due Sun 8/30, 11:59 PM. Three short lines, AI off.\n\nSemester Start Attestations (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1298699) â€” due Mon 8/31, 11:59 PM.\n\nSemester Start Pulse Check (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1298704) â€” due Mon 8/31, 11:59 PM. Five minutes, used for team formation.\n\nBest,\n\nProf. Wen Gu"
    },
    {
      "title": "Reminder: updated classroom â€” GFC W330",
      "posted_utc": "2026-08-26T20:09:53Z",
      "posted_local": "Wed Aug 26, 2026, 4:09 PM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1390304",
      "body": "A reminder that ISOM 352 now meets in GFC W330 (West wing of GBS).\n\nOur first class is Thursday, August 27:\n\nSection 1101: 10:00 AM â€“ 11:15 AM\n\nSection 1102: 11:30 AM â€“ 12:45 PM\n\nPlease leave a few extra minutes to find the room â€” GFC W330 is in the west wing of Goizueta.\n\nSee you tomorrow.\n\nBest,\n\nProf. Wen Gu"
    },
    {
      "title": "Welcome to ISOM 352 ADA",
      "posted_utc": "2026-08-26T04:14:25Z",
      "posted_local": "Wed Aug 26, 2026, 12:14 AM EDT",
      "url": "https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/discussion_topics/1389421",
      "body": "Hello and welcome to ISOM 352 Applied Data Analytics with Coding (ADA)!\n\nI hope you had a great summer break. My name is Prof. Wen Gu, an Associate Professor in the ISOM area of Goizueta. As we start the new term, I'm excited to dive into the world of coding with all of you and explore the world of data and its role in business decision-making. This course will cover a wide range of topics, including Python programming, data visualization and analytics, querying data with SQL, and an introduction to machine learning and artificial intelligence. We will also discuss how to implement analytics and how to effectively communicate findings.\n\nOur first class meeting is on Thursday 8/27 in Goizueta Room W330 (West Wing).\n\nSection 1101: 10:00 AM â€“ 11:15 AM\n\nSection 1102: 11:30 AM â€“ 12:45 PM\n\nPlease do the following before class.\n\n1) Read the syllabus (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/files/16826021) and tentative schedule (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/files/16897867). Both are also on the Syllabus tab (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/syllabus).\n2) Have a valid Google account (we use Google Colab).\n3) Update your OS: MacOS (https://support.apple.com/en-us/108382) or Windows (https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/get-the-latest-windows-update-7d20e88c-0568-483a-37bc-c3885390d212).\n\nThis week, please also complete two short Canvas items (due dates are on each assignment):\n\nSemester Start Pulse Check (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1298704)\n\nSemester Start Attestations (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/assignments/1298699)\n\nThe course homepage is the hub: Start Here (https://canvas.emory.edu/courses/165126/modules) for intro and setup. Peyton, the Virtual TA, lives on the home page â€” assistant, never author, and some class beats are screens-down.\n\nIn addition, here are the 4 strongly recommended textbooks (Don't worry. They are either free or very reasonably priced)\n\n[cs] Charles Severance â€œPython for Everybodyâ€ (ISBN-10: 1530051126)\n\nFree: https://www.py4e.com/book.php (https://www.py4e.com/book.php)\n\n[dc] Daniel Chen â€œPandas for Everyoneâ€ (ISBN-10: 0134546938)\n\nhttps://amzn.to/2U4juto (https://amzn.to/2U4juto)\n\n[ab] Alan Beaulieu, â€œLearning SQL: Generate, Manipulate, and Retrieve Dataâ€ (ISBN-10: 1492057614)\n\nhttps://amzn.to/38C0p5x (https://amzn.to/38C0p5x)\n\n[as] Al Sweigart, â€œAutomate the Boring Stuff with Pythonâ€ 2nd Edition (ISBN-10: 1593279922)\n\nFree: https://automatetheboringstuff.com/ (https://automatetheboringstuff.com/)\n\nI'm looking forward to a productive and engaging term with all of you. Let's get started!\n\nBest,\n\nProf. Wen Gu"
    }
  ]
}
